solution:
To find the sum, we look at the first few powers of 2 modulo 7: \begin{align*}
2^0 &\equiv 1, \\
2^1 &\equiv 2, \\
2^2 &\equiv 4, \\
2^3 &\equiv 8 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}
\end{align*}Since $2^3 \equiv 1 \pmod{7}$, the powers of 2 modulo 7 repeat in cycles of 3.  Therefore, \begin{align*}
&1 + 2 + 2^2 + 2^3 + \dots + 2^{100} \\
&\quad\equiv 1 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 2 + 4 + \dots + 1 + 2 + 4 + 1 + 2 \\
&\quad\equiv (1 + 2 + 4) + (1 + 2 + 4) + \dots + (1 + 2 + 4) + 1 + 2 \\
&\quad\equiv \boxed{3} \pmod{7}.
\end{align*}

problem:
A square is inscribed in a circle.  A smaller square has one side coinciding with a side of the larger square and has two vertices on the circle, as shown.  What percent of the area of the larger square is the area of the smaller square?

[asy]
