Note that $16=2^4$ is a power of two. We can use the rule $(a^b)^c = a^{bc}$ to find that \[16^2 = (2^4)^2 = 2^8.\]Now we return back to the original problem. After substituting in $16^2=2^8$, we use the rule $a^b \div a^c = a^{b-c}$: \begin{align*}
2^{16} \div 16^2 &= 2^{16} \div 2^8 \\
&= 2^{16-8} \\
&= 2^8 = \boxed{256}.
\end{align*}

We expand each product separately: \begin{align*}
(2x-5)(x+7) &= 2x(x) + 2x(7) -5(x) -5(7)\\
&=2x^2 +14x - 5x -35\\
&= 2x^2 +9x - 35
\end{align*}and \begin{align*}
(x+5)(2x-1) &=x(2x) + x(-1) +5(2x) + 5(-1)\\
&=2x^2 -x + 10x -5\\
&=2x^2 +9x - 5.
\end{align*}So, we have  \begin{align*}&\ \ \ \ (2x-5)(x+7) - (x+5)(2x-1) \\&= 2x^2+9x -35 - (2x^2 +9x -5) = \boxed{-30}.\end{align*}

